        </xs:sequence>
      </xs:extension>
    </xs:complexContent>
  </xs:complexType>
  <xs:simpleType name="BridgeCodeSimpleType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A data type for the kind of structure spanning and permitting passage over a river, chasm, road, or the like.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:string">
      <xs:enumeration value="ARCH">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Arch Bridge. A structure supported by an arch or series of arches, with the roadway passing through or over the arch. A fixed arch type is constructed without hinges (i.e., the ends are rigidly anchored at the abutments).  A hinged arch type has in its design one or more hinges permitting movement through the joint.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="BEAM">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Beam Bridge. A short span bridge, always deck type, with steel, concrete, or timberbeams extending between abutments or piers. Since this type of construction is under the deck of the bridge and cannot be seen from overhead imagery, it is grouped with girder types(i.e., beam/girder).</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="CABLE">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Cable Bridge. One in which planking can be easily emplaced or removed from a close series of cables spanning a depression or obstacle.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="CANTILEVER">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Cantilever Bridge. A structure of which one portion acts as an anchorage for sustaining another portion which projects beyond the supporting pier.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="DECK">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Deck Bridge. One in which the floor system is supported on or near the top of the trusses or girders with no more than a guardrail at the sides.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="FOOT">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Foot Bridge. One that spans a depression or obstacle, suitable only for human or animal foot traffic.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="GIRDER">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Girder Bridge. A bridge in which the main support is provided by girders. It may be either through or deck construction, and is usually described in terms of the girder type used (i.e., plate, lattice, etc.)  Since this type construction is under the deck of the bridge and cannot be seen from overhead imagery, it is grouped with girder types (i.e., beam/girder).</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="MIXED">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Mixed Bridge. Normally a multispan structure consisting of two or more span types.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="MOVABLE BASCULE">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Movable Bascule Bridge. One which moves in a vertical plane, rotating about a horizontal axis (trunnion type), or rolling back from the opening (rolling-lift type).</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="MOVABLE RETRACTABLE">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Movable Retractable Bridge. One which has a span capable of being retracted to a position normally atop the roadbed of the adjoining fixed span. These are most commonly associated with railroad bridges.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="MOVABLE SWING">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Movable Swing Bridge. One which revolves horizontally and generally consists of two or more plate girders or trusses supported on a pier in such a way that they can be rotated on a circular track or pivot resting on or attached to the pier.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="MOVABLE VERT-LIFT">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Movable Vert-Lift Bridge. One in which a span can be raised vertically to allow vessels to pass underneath.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="PONTOON">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Pontoon Bridge. A temporary bridge which is supported by light, flat-bottom boats or floats.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SEE REMARKS">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>See Remarks. Enter this code when the bridge or ferry is not covered by a type.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SUSPENSION">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Suspension Bridge. One with a roadway suspended from two or more cables passing over towers and securely anchored at the ends.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="TRAMWAY">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Tramway. One that has a rail for a tramcar.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="TRESTLE">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Trestle. A beam bridge supported by bents or towers, commonly used with railroads.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="TRUSS">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Truss Bridge. One in which the roadway loads are transmitted from the floor to the abutments or piers by means of trusses, usually one along each side.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="UNKNOWN">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Unknown. Self-explanatory</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>
