  <xs:simpleType name="DetectorCodeSimpleType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A data type for the detector type of a ground sensor.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:string">
      <xs:enumeration value="AC">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Acoustic. Detects the transmission of sound through various media or in various enclosures or conduits, including the effects of reflection, refraction, interference, diffraction, and absorption.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="BP">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Best Possible.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="CA">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Commandable Acoustic. Controllable by commands from other sources.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="EL">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Electronic. Detects the output of electron devices, circuits or systems utilizing electron devices, including electron tubes, magnetic amplifiers, transistors, and other devices that do the work of electron tubes.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="EM">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Electromagnetic. Detects radio (short-wave and ultra short-wave), infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, x-rays and gamma-rays waves.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="FL">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Forward Looking Infrared (FLIR). An imaging scanning electro-optical receiver which is mounted in the front of an airplane to cover the forward sector. The FLIR images on the basis of emitted target radiation due to the target's temperature as contrasted with its background. This contrast is the basis of passive imaging used in these FLIR systems.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="FR">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Frame Photography. Examples of this type of photography are photography taken of one complete coverage of a television picture, or of the rectangular area representing the size of copy handled by a facsimile system.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="GD">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Gas Detection. Gas detectors come in two basic types, combustion-type and thermal -conductivity type. The most commonly used combustion type employs a self-heated "hot wire" detector, usually platinum, which serves as a combustion catalyst. After the combustion process is started, a temperature sensor, such as a thermocouple, may detect the temperature rise which is a measure of the concentration of gas. Different gases vary considerably in their ability to conduct heat. These variations make it possible to determine the concentrations of a number of gases, the basis for a thermal-conductivity type gas detector.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="II">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Image Intensification, Including Night Vision Devices other than Strictly Optical. This device consists of: (1) a input, photoelectron-emitting photocathode, which converts the image scene photons into an electron image; (2) a drift space with an electrostatic field to accelerate the electrons (providing the amplification energy) and to image the accelerated electrons onto the output surface (i.e., also performs an electron optic function); and (3) a phosphor that converts these high-energy electrons into visible light. Therefore, this device simply provides optical gain, or amplification.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="IR">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Infrared. Radiant energy within the wavelength range 1 to 100 micrometer (10**-6 meter) is considered infrared energy. In general, unlike ultraviolet energy, infrared energy is not evaluated on a wavelength basis but rather in terms of all of such energy incident upon a surface. An infrared sensor would normally consist of a device for converting an invisible infrared image into a visible image. This device would consist of an infrared-sensitive, semi-transparent photocathode on one end of an evacuated envelope and a phosphor screen on the other, with an electrostatic lens system between the two.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="LL">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Low Light Level Television (LLLTV). Very low light level. Operation can be obtained by coupling an image intensifier (optical amplifier) tube with a camera tube. The image intensifier tube is used as the prime sensor and its output is coupled to the photocathode of the camera tube. Low light level television has many advantages for night military operations by enhancing battlefield surveillance and reconnaissance for both defense and attack.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="LS">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>LASER. An acronym for Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation. The device is identical in theory of operation to the MASER except it operates in the optical region of the electromagnetic spectrum, rather than in the microwave. This device provides amplification in the optical region.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="MG">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Magnetic. Senses the number of magnetic lines of force that are striking the detecting element.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="OP">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Optical. Uses the measurement of the intensity, spectral distribution, polarization, or other characteristics of light or of infrared or ultraviolet radiation, emitted by or reflected from an object or passing through some medium, to perform range measurements, surveillances and other measures.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="RF">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Radio Frequency. Monitors, measures, or detects use of radio frequency (RF) energy.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SA">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Seismic Acoustic. Acoustic or low frequency signal to determine earth movements.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SE">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Seismic. Measures or detects earth movements.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SL">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Side Looking Airborne Radar (SLAR). High-resolution airborne radar having antennas aimed to the side of the flight path; used to provide high-resolution strip maps with photograph-like detail, to map unfriendly territory while flying along its perimeter, and to detect submarine wake against a background of sea clutter.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SM">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Seismic Magnetic. Disturbances in the earth's magnetic field used to detect earth movements.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="TI">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Thermal Imagery. Measuring and imaging of radiant energy within the visible portion of the electromagnetic spectrum.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="TV">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Television. The transmission of still or motion scenes, by electrical means, for instantaneous viewing.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="VV">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Video. The streaming or full-motion video device for instantaneous viewing.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>
