        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Deliberate. Used when there is time to plan, organize and prepare logistical support.  Static installation protection.  Part of perimeter security of depots, airfields, static missile sites, etc.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="HASTY">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Hasty. Temporary combat minelaying is done to provide security to our units.  Used to block, disrupt, or canalize enemy attacks.  Mines are usually located near the Forward Edge of the Battle Area (FEBA). They are easily laid and recovered.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SCATTER">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Scatterable. Laid without regard to classical pattern.  Used in both offense and defense.  Able to lay a large number of mines quickly with minimum manpower.  Delivered by artillery, aircraft, ground dispenser, or thrown by hand.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SPECIAL">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Special. Used in mining of railroads, routes, streambeds, and other special targets.  Minelaying technique may differ from cross-country mining.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
