  <xs:simpleType name="SurfaceFirmnessCodeSimpleType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A data type for a determination of the amount of support a surface will provide for vehicles, personnel, or equipment.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:string">
      <xs:enumeration value="FIRM AT ALL TIMES">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Firm at All Times. The surface material is firm at all times consisting of a well-graded mixture of particle sizes. Beach material will support the weight of wheeled vehicles and will allow initial vehicle movement without special equipment aids.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="FIRM WHEN DRY">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Firm When Dry. The surface material is firm when dry and will support the weight of wheeled vehicles without excessive vehicle settling and/or loss of traction. Beach material will allow initial vehicle movement without special equipment aids.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="FIRM WHEN WET">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Firm When Wet. The surface material is firm when wet and will support the weight of wheeled vehicles without excessive vehicle settling and/or loss of traction. Beach material will allow initial vehicle movement without special equipment aids.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="LOOSE">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Loose. The surface material is generally composed of poorly graded (limited size range) materials which lack fine material to fill the void. Beach material will support the weight of wheeled vehicles, but provides for poor traction. Pebbles and cobbles are examples of poorly graded materials.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SOFT AT ALL TIMES">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Soft at All Times. The surface material is soft at all times. Generally the material is composed of dry sand or sand mixed with silt which will not support the weight of moving wheeled vehicles. Because of poor traction, wheeled vehicles will generally require the assistance of tracked vehicles or mats for initial movement.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SOFT WHEN DRY">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Soft When Dry. The surface material is soft when dry, may support the weight of wheeled vehicle, but because of poor traction, wheeled vehicles generally require the assistance of tracked vehicles or mats for initial movement. Soft beach materials are composed of dry sand, or sand mixed with silt.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SOFT WHEN WET">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Soft When Wet. The surface material is soft when wet. Material will not support the weight of moving wheeled vehicles.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="UNKNOWN">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Unknown. Surface material and firmness is unknown.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>
