  <xs:simpleType name="TaskGroupingCategoryCodeSimpleType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A data type for the criteria used to group related tasks in the ATO.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:string">
      <xs:enumeration value="AAR">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Air-to-Air Refueling. The objective of Air-to-Air Refueling (AAR) operations is to enhance combat effectiveness by extending the range, payload, or endurance of receiver aircraft.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="ABCC">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Airborne Command and Control. Airborne Command &amp; Control Communications (ABCCC) aircraft are specially equipped and manned by trained control and coordination personnel. They contribute to the command and control of many different types of air operation.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="AEW">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Airborne Early Warning. Airborne Early Warning &amp; Control (AEW&amp;C) aircraft are specially equipped and manned by trained control and coordination personnel. They contribute to the command and control of many different types of air operation.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="AI">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Air Interdiction. Air Interdiction (AI) is conducted to destroy, neutralize or delay the enemy's military potential before it can be brought to bear effectively against friendly forces at such distance from friendly forces that detailed integration of each air mission with the fire and movement of friendly forces is not required.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="AIR RECCE">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Air Reconnaissance. The collection of information of intelligence interest either by visual observation from the air or through the use of airborne sensors.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="AIR SURV">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Air Surveillance. The systematic observation of airspace by electronic, visual, or other means, primarily for the purpose of identifying and determining the movements of aircraft and missiles, friendly, and enemy, in the air space under observation.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="AMB SUP">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Amphibious Ops Support. Air support for amphibious operations can be provided by both land and sea based air assets depending upon the location of the Amphibious Objective Area (AOA). Air support will normally include the Air Interdiction (AI) and Close Air Support (CAS) roles of air-land operations, but may also require counter-air and supporting air operations.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="AO">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Airborne Operations. Air delivered combat power can be used to seize ground or installations that are vital to the enemy by delivering land forces directly onto the objective. This may be achieved by parachute insertion (airborne) or Tactical Air Landed Operation (TALO). Ports of entry, airfields, and key installations or structures, all offer potential objectives.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="ASACS">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Air Surveillance and Control System. A network of fixed, mobile, and airborne radars, associated communications and facilities that provide for the detection, recognition, reporting, and control of interception and engagement of airborne vehicles within the detection range.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="ASUW">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Antisurface Warfare. The aim of Antisurface Warfare (ASUW) is to prevent an adversary from effectively employing his surface forces. Aircraft, submarines, or surface vessels can carry out this task, preferably in a coordinated action. ASUW covers a wide range of operations involving surveillance and reconnaissance missions that may culminate in the targeting and attack of enemy vessels.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="ASW">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Antisubmarine Warfare. The aim of Anti-Submarine Warfare (ASW) is to deny the adversary effective use of submarines.  Countering the submarine threat demands an extensive range of capabilities and may involve the use of fixed-wing Maritime Patrol Aircraft (MPA), helicopters, friendly submarines, surface ships, or other aircraft.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="AT">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Air Transport. Air Transport (AT), commonly known as airlift, provides a military commander with the capability to deploy, employ, and redeploy forces and equipment quickly and over considerable distances, sustain those forces and support effective application of their military effort. AT operations are considered in two categories: strategic (inter-theater) and theater (intra-theater).</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="CAS">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Close Air Support. Close Air Support (CAS) is defined as air action against hostile targets which are in close proximity to friendly forces and which require detailed integration of each air mission with the fire and movement of those forces.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="COMAO">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Combined Air Operations. Combined Air Operations (COMAO) are designed to maximize the impact of a given force by concentrating its effect, whilst at the same time minimizing attrition through mutual support within the attacking force and by saturating defenses en-route, and in the target area. To achieve its aim, a COMAO may need Air-to-Air Refueling (AAR), Air Defense (AD), reconnaissance, surveillance, targeting designation, jamming, and defense suppression aircraft in support.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="CSAR">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Combat Search and Rescue. Combat Search and Rescue (CSAR) is a coordinated operation using pre-established procedures for the detection, location, identification, and rescue of downed aircrew in hostile territory in crisis or wartime and, when appropriate, of isolated personnel in distress, who are trained and equipped to be rescued.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="DCA">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Defensive Counter Air. Defensive Counter Air (DCA) involves destruction or neutralization of enemy forces conducted near to, or over, friendly territory and are generally reactive to the initiative of enemy air assets. DCA includes the employment of active air defense weapon systems such as fighters, surface-to-air missiles, and anti-aircraft artillery, complemented by passive defense measures.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="EW">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Electronic Warfare. Electronic Warfare (EW) is the employment of the full range of modern communications, surveillance, and weapon systems operating across the electromagnetic spectrum, and in an attempt to dominate the spectrum, whilst denying its use to the enemy.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="INTERCEPT">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Air Defense Interception. An intercept mission involves the use of an aircraft to intercept enemy or unknown aircraft. Upon completion of the intercept other actions may be ordered e.g. visual identification (interrogation), shadowing, diversion of the target from its intended flight path or mission (intervention) or engagement.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="OCA">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Offensive Counter Air. Offensive Counter Air (OCA) involves destruction or neutralization of enemy forces mostly ranging throughout enemy territory and generally conducted at the initiative of the friendly force. OCA may include fighter sweep, fighter escort, airfield attack, and attacks against missiles and associated systems on the ground, including Suppression of Enemy Air Defenses (SEAD).</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="PACKAGE">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Package. Force packaging is used if the requirement is to concentrate a large composite force against a single objective, and particularly if there is a need for combat support forces. The optimum combination of impact and survivability may only be achieved if the force operates as an integrated unit.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SA">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Strategic Attack. Strategic Attack is carried out to defeat an adversary's center of gravity or attack other vital target sets including command elements, energy sources, vital war production assets, and key supporting infrastructure to achieve the desired effects. It is normally conducted beyond the range of surface force assets.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SAO">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Special Air Operations. Special Air Operations (SAO) support special operations and may be conducted during peace, crisis and conflict. SAO activities may include both combat and non-combat tasks involving the deployment, infiltration, support, exfiltration, and withdrawal of special forces.  SAO may be conducted in conjunction with other joint operations or may be autonomous.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SAR">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Search and Rescue. The use of aircraft, surface craft, submarines, specialized rescue teams and equipment to search for and rescue personnel in distress on land or at sea.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="SAW">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Surface-to-Air Weapons. Surface-to-Air Weapons (SAW), comprising surface-to-air missiles and anti-aircraft artillery, is an integral part of a Defensive Counter Air (DCA) mission.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
      <xs:enumeration value="UNIT">
        <xs:annotation>
          <xs:documentation>Unit Tasking. Unit tasking provides the capability to task all missions of one unit in single section of the ATO mission file. This capability also provides multi-role aircraft units a single section of the ATO for their unit mission tasking.</xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
      </xs:enumeration>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>
